course: dictionary
lesson: 4017
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. The word gas is a good example. «The gas stove was hot.» The words stove and hot show that gas here means a fuel that burns, used for cooking.
explain: «Air is a mixture of gases, such as nitrogen and oxygen.» Nothing in that sentence is about cooking or cars. Here gases means the kind of stuff in the air: a substance with no fixed shape that spreads out to fill any space.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. In «Burning coal, oil and gas adds carbon dioxide to the air», gas is a fuel. Does "burning a fuel adds carbon dioxide to the air" still make sense? Yes, so you have chosen the right meaning.
explain: Some meanings depend on where you live. In America and Canada, gas can mean petrol, the fuel for cars. A British reader would say petrol, so when you meet an unfamiliar meaning, use the clues in the text and check a dictionary if you are still unsure.
fact: Gas is one of the three main states of matter, along with solid and liquid.
fact: «Gas» means a substance like air that has no fixed shape and spreads out to fill any space.
fact: «Gas» can also mean a fuel that burns, as in «a gas stove», used for cooking and heating.
fact: In America and Canada, «gas» means petrol, the fuel for cars.
fact: «Gas» can mean air in the stomach, as in «A tummy rumble is gas and liquid squeezed along the gut.»
exercise: «Water becomes a gas when it boils.» What does gas mean here? | A fuel that burns, used for cooking | Air in the stomach | *A substance like air that spreads out to fill any space | Petrol for a car
exercise: «The gas stove was hot, so we stayed back.» What does gas mean here? | Petrol for a car | Air in the stomach | *A fuel that burns, used for cooking and heating | A cold, wet mist
exercise: «In America, a driver fills the car with gas.» What does gas mean here? | A substance like air | Something that is hot | *Petrol, the fuel for cars | Air in the stomach
test: «If you smell gas, don't touch switches, get out and tell an adult.» What does gas mean here? | A loud noise | *A fuel gas that can leak from a pipe, such as natural gas | A sweet smell from flowers | A fuel gas that can leak from a pipe
test: «A tummy rumble is gas and liquid squeezed along the gut.» What does gas mean here? | Petrol for a car | A substance that is solid | *Air in the stomach | A fuel that burns on a stove
exam: Jayden meets the word gas in a text and is not sure which meaning is right. What should he do first? | Skip the sentence and never come back to it | Guess the meaning he knows best and move on | *Read the words around it and try each meaning in the sentence | Ask someone to read the text aloud for him
